In our previous discussion, James requested a classification of several countries based on their income groups: low, lower-middle, upper-middle, and high. I provided the classifications using World Bank criteria, detailing that no countries fell into the low-income category. India was classified as lower-middle income, while Brazil, China, Mexico, Russia, and South Africa were categorized as upper-middle income. Canada, France, Japan, and the United States were identified as high-income countries. I noted that these classifications can evolve with changes in economic conditions.
